gameinput: a pad is also a generic controller and a UI navigation device, and macOS's own pads when XInput has none

MetalHorizon 1.1.1, issue #15 (a DualSense that Forza Horizon 6 did not react to). Two causes, one fix each:
- A pad was only a gamepad here. Windows reports an Xbox pad as a gamepad, a generic controller (axes, buttons, the
  D-pad as a switch) and a UI navigation device, and a game may look for it as either: a racing game that also takes
  wheels may ask for generic controllers, and menus are often read through UI navigation. Both are now answered: six
  axes, ten buttons and an eight-way switch, each described in the device information, and the UI navigation buttons
  mapped as Windows maps a gamepad's.
- A pad on Bluetooth never reached XInput: Wine's HID bus runs in winedevice.exe, which has no Cocoa run loop, and
  there SDL's macOS joystick driver sees no Bluetooth pad. When XInput has no pad at all, the pads macOS itself knows
  are now read in the game's process through GameController.framework (dlls/gameinput/unixlib.c, loaded at run time
  and messaged through the Objective-C runtime): DualSense, DualShock 4, Xbox, Switch Pro and MFi pads, on Bluetooth
  as on a cable. WINE_GAMEINPUT_APPLE=0 turns that source off. Rumble stays XInput's.
Tested without the game: engine/tests/gameinput-pads.

